  title="The Annual Maintenance Checklist That Protects Your Major Systems"
  description="A season-by-season maintenance calendar for Middle Tennessee properties: HVAC, roof, water heater, gutters, and exterior, with what each task prevents."
>
  <p>
    Major systems rarely die suddenly. Roofs, HVAC systems, water heaters,
    and exteriors fail slowly, in ways that are cheap to catch and expensive
    to ignore. The owners who get 20 years out of a roof and 15 out of an
    HVAC system are not lucky. They run a calendar. Here is the one we run
    across 350+ Middle Tennessee units, organized by season.
  </p>

  <h2>Spring: get ahead of cooling season and storm season</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HVAC cooling tune-up.</strong> Coils, refrigerant, capacitors, condensate drain. Prevents the July failure and the ceiling stain under the air handler. More detail in our <a href="/articles/annual-hvac-service/">annual HVAC service guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Roof inspection.</strong> Middle Tennessee spring storms find every weak shingle and lifted flashing. A documented inspection catches damage while it is a repair, not a ceiling replacement, and gives you the photo record insurance claims need.</li>
    <li><strong>Gutter cleaning and downspout check.</strong> Clogged gutters push water into fascia, foundations, and crawl spaces. Water always wins eventually, so give it somewhere to go.</li>
    <li><strong>Exterior caulk and paint check.</strong> Failed caulk around windows, doors, and trim is how rot starts. A tube of caulk now beats replacing trim boards later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Summer: the outdoor structures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deck and fence inspection.</strong> Check railings, posts, and boards while everything is dry. Stain or seal on schedule, since it roughly doubles the life of the wood.</li>
    <li><strong>Tree trimming.</strong> Limbs over the roof are future roof repairs and gutter cloggers. Trim them back before winter ice makes them heavier.</li>
    <li><strong>Irrigation and drainage check.</strong> Water pooling against a foundation in summer becomes a crawl space problem by winter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fall: get ahead of heating season</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HVAC heating tune-up.</strong> Heat exchanger, burners, blower, and safety checks before the first freeze, when every HVAC company's phone starts ringing at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Water heater flush and anode check.</strong> Sediment shortens tank life and steals efficiency. A flush is cheap. A failed tank on the second floor is not.</li>
    <li><strong>Gutter cleaning, round two.</strong> After the leaves drop. This is the one everyone skips and the one that matters most.</li>
    <li><strong>Winterization.</strong> Exterior faucets, hose bibs, and vulnerable pipe runs. One February freeze pays for a decade of winterization visits.</li>
    <li><strong>Smoke and CO detector testing.</strong> Batteries and function, every unit, every fall. In rentals this is a compliance item, not a suggestion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Winter: the indoor pass</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filter audit.</strong> HVAC filters on schedule all year, but winter is when clogged filters strain furnaces hardest.</li>
    <li><strong>Under-sink and supply line check.</strong> Slow leaks under sinks and behind toilets do their damage invisibly. Five minutes with a flashlight per unit finds them.</li>
    <li><strong>Caulk and grout check in wet areas.</strong> Failed shower grout feeds water into wall cavities every single day it goes unfixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Door and window seal check.</strong> Comfort, utility bills, and tenant satisfaction, all in a tube of weatherstripping.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why a calendar beats a to-do list</h2>
  <p>
    None of these tasks is difficult. What makes them powerful is that they
    happen on schedule, every year, whether or not anything looks wrong,
    because the whole point is catching problems that do not look like
    anything yet. That is also why they rarely get done: no single task ever
    feels urgent. The fix is taking the decision out of it, either with a
    recurring calendar you actually honor or a
    <a href="/services/">maintenance partner</a> who runs the schedule for
    you, documents every visit, and flags what is aging before it fails.
  </p>
  <p>
